.TH SPLIT 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
split \- split a file into pieces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B split
.RB [ \-l
.IR line_count \ |
.B \-b
.IR n [ k | m ]]
.RB [ \-a
.IR suffix_length ]
.RI [ file
.RI [ name ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B split
reads
.I file
(or standard input, if
.I file
is omitted or is
.BR \- )
and writes it out as a sequence of consecutively-named output files,
each one piece of the input.
.PP
Output files are named by appending a suffix to
.IR name ,
starting at as many copies of the letter
.B a
as the suffix length and incrementing like a base-26 odometer:
.BR aa ,
.BR ab ,
\&.\|.\|.\&
.BR az ,
.BR ba ,
and so on. If more pieces are produced than the current suffix
length can name, this is reported as an error rather than wrapping
back around and overwriting an earlier piece.
.PP
Exactly one output file is always created for a given input, even
when the input is empty.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-l " line_count"
Split into pieces of
.I line_count
lines each (the last piece may be shorter). This is the default mode,
with
.I line_count
of 1000, when neither
.B \-l
nor
.B \-b
is given.
.TP
.BI \-b " n[k|m]"
Split into pieces of
.I n
bytes each (the last piece may be shorter), instead of splitting by
line count.
.I n
may be followed by
.B k
to multiply it by 1024, or
.B m
to multiply it by 1,048,576.
.B \-l
and
.B \-b
are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.BI \-a " suffix_length"
Use
.I suffix_length
letters for each output file's suffix, instead of the default of 2.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
The pathname of the file to split. If omitted, or given as
.BR \- ,
standard input is used.
.TP
.I name
The prefix for each output file's name. The default is the single
character
.BR x .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The input was split successfully.
.TP
.B >0
An error occurred: an invalid option or option argument, conflicting
.B \-l
and
.BR \-b ,
too many pieces for the suffix length, an unreadable input file, or a
write failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR csplit (1),
.BR cat (1)
